"Microsoft Word - psyarxiv.doc (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.10421v1)\nciteturn30883view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 5; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn30882view2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 138\nL0@P0: Are Language Models Models?\nL1@P0: Philip Resnik\nL2@P0: Department of Linguistics and Institute for Advanced Computer Studies\nL3@P0: University of Maryland\nL4@P0: resnik@umd.edu\nL5@P0: Commentary on Futrell, R. & Mahowald, K. (in press). How Linguistics Learned to Stop Worrying and\nL6@P0: Love the Language Models. Behavioral and Brain Sciences. http://doi.org/10.1017/S0140525X2510112X\nL7@P0: Abstract\nL8@P0: Futrell and Mahowald claim LMs “serve as model systems”, but an assessment at each of Marr’s three\nL9@P0: levels suggests the claim is clearly not true at the implementation level, poorly motivated at the\nL10@P0: algorithmic-representational level, and problematic at the computational theory level. LMs are good\nL11@P0: candidates as tools; calling them cognitive models overstates the case and unnecessarily feeds LLM hype.\nL12@P0: For a previous talk closely related to the discussion in this commentary, see: Resnik, P. What Are\nL13@P0: Large Language Models Models Of? Presentation at Dagstuhl Seminar 25301, Dagstuhl, Germany.\nL14@P0-1: July 21, 2025. https://youtu.be/Gt7IAT9lgrQ. [Video, 26min]It’s impossible to resist pointing out that in Stanley Kubrick’s 1964 film Dr. Strangelove, which inspired\nL15@P1: Futrell and Mahowald’s article title, the central theme involved people not thinking things through\nL16@P1: carefully enough, leading to a global apocalypse. I’m not worried about quite that level of catastrophe,\nL17@P1: mind you, but I do think the article risks further feeding into to a pervasive and ultimately harmful\nL18@P1: misperception about the relationship between language models (LMs) and human language processing—\nL19@P1: namely, the too-readily overextended idea of LMs as “model systems” alluded to in the article’s abstract.\nL20@P1: What do we mean when we say “model system”? A model, at any level of explanation, is an embodiment\nL21@P1: of theory that, in addition to capturing generalizations and licensing verifiable inferences and\nL22@P1: predictions—requirements of any theory—also contains structured correspondences between entities and\nL23@P1: relationships in the model and corresponding entities and relationships in the real-world system being\nL24@P1: modeled (Frigg and Nguyen 2017). Notwithstanding its imperfections, Marr (1982) remains a widely\nL25@P1: accepted scaffolding (Peebles and Cooper, 2015), so let’s consider the status of and prospects for LMs as\nL26@P1: models of language processing at each of Marr’s three levels.\nL27@P1: First, the implementation level, on which we needn’t spend much time. The authors argue for a\nL28@P1: hypothesis-generation role, but they’re also clearly aware that functional parallels are not implementation\u0002level models. Indeed, the connectionism underlying today’s LMs was founded on the abandonment of\nL29@P1: implementation-level biological correspondences in favor of function optimization. In Fridman (2021),\nL30@P1: Jay McClelland recalls Geoff Hinton successfully arguing, “The problem with the way you guys have\nL31@P1: been approaching this is that you’ve been looking for inspiration from biology…That’s the wrong way to\nL32@P1: go about it.” Consequently, the dominant LM research today demonstrates little substantive engagement\nL33@P1: with the numerous relevant advances made in implementation-level understanding of the brain since\nL34@P1: connectionism’s loose 20th-century inspirations (e.g., Hickok and Poeppel, 2007; Barrett and Satpute,\nL35@P1: 2013; Hawkins and Ahmad, 2016; Wright et al., 2025; for higher-level reviews see Pulvermüller, 2021,\nL36@P1: 2023 and Durstewitz et al., 2025).\nL37@P1: Algorithmic/representational level. The authors’ argument here hinges on convergence, that systems\nL38@P1: solving the same hard problem are driven toward similar mechanisms (Cao and Yamins, 2024). But\nL39@P1: unlike visual object recognition, a key part of what makes “language” hard is that it decomposes into very\nL40@P1: distinct subproblems, not merely competing constraints within a single problem. In addition, mechanistic\nL41@P1: exceptions to convergence are easily found in biology (e.g. sharks and dolphins facing ecologically\nL42@P1: similar prey-capture problems evolved markedly different mechanisms, relying on electroreception and\nL43@P1: echolocation, respectively) and in computation (e.g. iterative and recursive computations of the Fibonacci\nL44@P1: sequence). In medical research, model-organism choices are supported by existing, solidly established\nL45@P1: mechanistic evidence; e.g., mice and ferrets are preferred in work on tumerogenesis and influenza,\nL46@P1: respectively—not vice versa—because mice share oncogene pathways with humans and ferrets share lung\nL47@P1: physiology (Bedell et al., 1997; Belser et al., 2011).\nL48@P1: Consider the following scenario. Two aliens from another galaxy, whom I’ll dub Abbot and Costello after\nL49@P1: Villeneuve (2016), show up on earth with their own billions of years of evolution behind them. In a year\nL50@P1: they absorb nearly everything ever written, and become phenomenal conversationalists; moreover, they\nL51@P1: generously offer to let human scientists experiment and probe their brain-boxes to our hearts’ content.\nL52@P1: Here’s the question: why in the world would one expect to find mechanistic correspondences between\nL53@P1: what’s happening in Abbot and Costello’s boxes and what’s happening in the human cognitive apparatus?\nL54@P1: Nonetheless, for LMs, the “argument from amazingness” is pervasive—if they are doing something so\nL55@P1: very human so very well, surely they must be operating under the hood in human-like ways?\nL56@P1: As I’ve highlighted, the argument is unreliable at best, and, moreover, it just isn’t necessary in order for\nL57@P1: LMs to contribute value to computational cognitive modeling. Giulianelli et al. (2024), for example, use\nL58@P1-2: LLMs to generate a sample of continuations, in order to operationalize a generalization of surprisal that captures distinctions between anticipatory and responsive online processes in comprehension. This use\nL59@P2: depends only on generation of plausible text, LMs’ undisputed strength (for better or worse; see Resnik,\nL60@P2: 2025), without requiring claims about their cognitive plausibility. Similarly, LMs can provide useful\nL61@P2: proxies (with caveats; see Oh and Schuler, 2023) for human comprehenders’ next-word probabilities\nL62@P2: when operationalizing models where such probabilities play a role, e.g. Hahn et al.’s (2022) integration of\nL63@P2: expectation- and memory-based theories of processing difficulty. LMs are a promising source of world\nL64@P2: knowledge for hierarchical predictive coding models, which are neurobiologically plausible and have\nL65@P2: recently been applied to language comprehension (Nour Eddine et al., 2024; Ohams et al., 2026).\nL66@P2: Computational theory level. Ironically, many who “love the language models” seem to exhibit the same\nL67@P2: physics envy that you find in Chomskian theory, idealizing a uniform, elegant computation-level\nL68@P2: paradigm for a wide range of phenomena. But evolution is a scruffy tinkerer, not a neat software engineer\nL69@P2: (Jacob, 1977). It doesn’t re-design from the bottom up when it produces new functionality. For LMs, just\nL70@P2: as for Chomsky, the drive to explain “du visible compliqué par de l’invisible simple” (Perrin, 1913)\nL71@P2: creates a problematic gap: even if these architectures can approximate diverse linguistic phenomena, and\nL72@P2: even if correlations are found between one black box and the other, ultimately there remains the burden of\nL73@P2: mapping any such computational theory to a biologically plausible algorithmic account of real-time\nL74@P2: processes distributed over diverse, specialized components. And there are plenty of other less problematic\nL75@P2: computational-theory games in town (e.g., Eliasmith, 2013; Suárez et al., 2024; Sprevak and Smith, 2023).\nL76@P2: At all levels we do need a pluralistic approach, and LMs can be tremendously productive as tools in\nL77@P2: support of developing explanatory models. But LMs themselves are poor candidates as “model systems”\nL78@P2: at any of Marr’s three levels. The distinction matters, for how we prioritize research questions, interpret\nL79@P2: results, and communicate findings to neighboring fields. For a whole variety of reasons, large language\nL80@P2: models are the subject of enormous hype. Let’s not undermine some of the more nuanced arguments in\nL81@P2: this paper by overstating the case.\nL82@P2: Acknowledgments\nL83@P2: I’m grateful for helpful comments from Katherine Howitt, Colin Phillips, and Samer Nour Eddine. Any\nL84@P2: errors or deficiencies are entirely my own.\nL85@P2: References\nL86@P2: Barrett, L. F., & Satpute, A. B. (2013). Large-scale brain networks in affective and social neuroscience:\nL87@P2: Towards an integrative functional architecture of the brain. Current Opinion in Neurobiology, 23(3),\nL88@P2: 361–372.\nL89@P2: Bedell, M. A., Jenkins, N. A., & Copeland, N. G. (1997). Mouse models of human disease. Part I:\nL90@P2: techniques and resources for genetic analysis in mice. Genes & development, 11(1), 1–10.\nL91@P2: https://doi.org/10.1101/gad.11.1.1\nL92@P2: Belser, J. A., Katz, J. M., & Tumpey, T. M. (2011). The ferret as a model organism to study influenza A\nL93@P2: virus infection. Disease models & mechanisms, 4(5), 575–579. https://doi.org/10.1242/dmm.007823\nL94@P2: Cao, R., & Yamins, D. (2024). Explanatory models in neuroscience, Part 2: Functional intelligibility and\nL95@P2-3: the contravariance principle. Cognitive Systems Research, 85, 101200.Durstewitz, D., Averbeck, B., & Koppe, G. (2025). What neuroscience can tell AI about learning in\nL96@P3: continuously changing environments. Nature Machine Intelligence, 1-16. https://doi.org/10.1038/s42256-\nL97@P3: 025-01146-z\nL98@P3: Eliasmith, C. (2013). How to build a brain: A neural architecture for biological cognition. Oxford\nL99@P3: University Press.\nL100@P3: Fridman, L. (2021, September 28). Geoffrey Hinton is a genius | Jay McClelland and Lex\nL101@P3: Fridman [Video]. YouTube. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lLBbsif2Xt4\nL102@P3: Frigg, R., & Nguyen, J. (2017). Models and representation. In L. Magnani & T. Bertolotti\nL103@P3: (Eds.), Springer handbook of model-based science (pp. 49–102). Springer International Publishing.\nL104@P3: Giulianelli, M., Opedal, A., & Cotterell, R. (2024). Generalized measures of anticipation and\nL105@P3: responsivity in online language processing. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics:\nL106@P3: EMNLP 2024 (pp. 11648–11669). Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL107@P3: Hahn, M., Futrell, R., Levy, R., & Gibson, E. (2022). A resource-rational model of human processing of\nL108@P3: recursive linguistic structure. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 119(43), e2122602119.\nL109@P3: Hawkins, J., & Ahmad, S. (2016). Why neurons have thousands of synapses, a theory of sequence\nL110@P3: memory in neocortex. Frontiers in neural circuits, 10, 23.\nL111@P3: Hickok, G., & Poeppel, D. (2007). The cortical organization of speech processing. Nature Reviews\nL112@P3: Neuroscience, 8(5), 393–402.\nL113@P3: Jacob, F. (1977). Evolution and tinkering. Science, 196(4295), 1161-1166.\nL114@P3: Marr, D. (1982). Vision: A computational investigation into the human representation and processing of\nL115@P3: visual information. W. H. Freeman.\nL116@P3: Nour Eddine, S., Brothers, T., Wang, L., Spratling, M., & Kuperberg, G. R. (2024). A predictive coding\nL117@P3: model of the N400. Cognition, 246, 105755.\nL118@P3: Oh, B., & Schuler, W. (2023). Why does surprisal from larger transformer-based language models\nL119@P3: provide a poorer fit to human reading times? Transactions of the Association for Computational\nL120@P3: Linguistics, 11, 336–350.\nL121@P3: Ohams, C., Nair, S., Bhattasali, S., & Resnik, P. (2026). A predictive coding model for online sentence\nL122@P3: processing. Journal of Memory and Language, 146, 104705.\nL123@P3: Peebles, D., & Cooper, R. P. (2015). Thirty years after Marr’s Vision: Levels of analysis in cognitive\nL124@P3: science. Topics in Cognitive Science, 7(2), 187–190.\nL125@P3: Perrin, J. (1913). Les Atomes. Paris: Félix Alcan.\nL126@P3: Pulvermüller, F., Tomasello, R., Henningsen-Schomers, M. R., et al. (2021). Biological constraints on\nL127@P3: neural network models of cognitive function. Nature Reviews Neuroscience, 22, 488–502.\nL128@P3: Pulvermüller, F. (2023). Neurobiological mechanisms for language, symbols and concepts: Clues from\nL129@P3-4: brain-constrained deep neural networks. Progress in Neurobiology, 230, 102511.Resnik, P. (2025). Large language models are biased because they are large language\nL130@P4: models. Computational Linguistics, 51(3), 885–906.\nL131@P4: Sprevak, M., & Smith, R. (2023). An introduction to predictive processing models of perception and\nL132@P4: decision‐making. Topics in Cognitive Science.\nL133@P4: Suárez, L. E., Mihalik, A., Milisav, F., Marshall, K., Li, M., Vértes, P. E., ... & Misic, B. (2024).\nL134@P4: Connectome-based reservoir computing with the conn2res toolbox. Nature Communications, 15(1), 656.\nL135@P4: Villeneuve, D. (Director). (2016). Arrival. [Film]. Paramount Pictures.\nL136@P4: Wright, W. J., Hedrick, N. G., & Komiyama, T. (2025). Distinct synaptic plasticity rules operate across\nL137@P4: dendritic compartments in vivo during learning. Science, 388(6744), 322-328.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntern